---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE, AUTHOR } from '../consts';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: AUTHOR.name,
    url: new URL('/about', SITE.url).href,
    image: new URL(AUTHOR.image, SITE.url).href,
    description:
      'Founder and author of Honest Picture, a research-backed library that places real-life numbers inside real population data.',
    mainEntityOfPage: new URL('/about', SITE.url).href,
    worksFor: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    name: 'About Honest Picture',
    url: new URL('/about', SITE.url).href,
    about: { '@type': 'Person', name: AUTHOR.name },
  },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="About Daniel Hernandez and Honest Picture"
  description="The story behind Honest Picture: who Daniel Hernandez is, the night that started the project, and the rules every page on this site has to pass."
  path="/about"
  ogImage="/og/page--about.png"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  crumb="About"
  eyebrow="About"
  heading="The story behind the mirror."
  lede="Honest Picture is researched, written, and run by one person. This page is the honest version of why it exists, plus the rules every page has to follow."
>
  <div class="author-card">
    <img src={AUTHOR.image} alt="Daniel Hernandez" width="96" height="96" />
    <div>
      <p class="author-card__name">{AUTHOR.name}</p>
      <p class="author-card__role">
        Founder of {SITE.name}. Creator of every page in the library.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Where this started</h2>
  <p>
    A few years ago, on an ordinary Tuesday night, I typed a question into a
    search engine that I was too embarrassed to ask anyone out loud: how much
    should I have saved by now? A close friend had just bought a house. Another
    had posted about a promotion. I was sitting in a rented apartment doing the
    math on my own life, and the math felt like losing.
  </p>
  <p>
    What I found that night made it worse. Half the results were financial
    companies telling me I should already have multiples of my salary put away.
    The other half were motivational posts telling me not to compare myself to
    anyone, which is easy to say and impossible to do. Nobody would just show me
    the actual numbers. Not the aspirational targets. The real ones. What people
    my age actually had.
  </p>
  <p>
    It took me weeks of digging to find the answer, buried in a Federal Reserve
    survey table that most people will never open. The real median was nothing
    like the number in my head. I remember the feeling very clearly, and it was
    not triumph. It was something quieter: the standard I had been failing
    against did not exist. I had been measuring my life with a broken ruler.
  </p>

  <h2>The spreadsheet that would not stop growing</h2>
  <p>
    That night turned into a habit. Every time a worry surfaced, I went looking
    for the population data behind it. How much do people actually sleep? How
    many close friends does a normal adult have? How often do couples argue?
    How many people actually love their job? I kept the answers in a
    spreadsheet, with a link to the source next to every number, because I
    stopped trusting any figure I could not trace.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pattern repeated so often it stopped being a surprise. Almost every
    time, the honest number was more forgiving than the number in my head. Not
    because life is easy, but because my sense of "everyone else" was built
    from the most visible, most polished slice of other people's lives. The
    data kept describing a quieter, more ordinary, more crowded middle than the
    one I imagined I was falling out of.
  </p>
  <p>
    I started sending the spreadsheet to friends when they spiraled about
    money or milestones. The response was almost always the same two
    sentences: "wait, really?" and then "why does nobody just show this?"
    Eventually I ran out of excuses for why nobody did. So I built it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why I made this project</h2>
  <p>
    Honest Picture exists because the question underneath all those searches,
    the one almost everyone carries and almost nobody says out loud, is: am I
    okay? And the two industries that answer it both have something to sell.
    The comparison machine sells you inadequacy, because inadequacy scrolls and
    clicks and buys. The self-help machine sells you rescue from that
    inadequacy. Neither one will simply hand you the base rates and let you
    decide how you feel.
  </p>
  <p>
    I wanted a third thing to exist. A place that treats you like an adult:
    here is the real distribution, here is where the research is strong, here
    is where it is mixed or thin, here is what actually helps, and here is
    what provably does not. No grade at the end. No program to buy. Just the
    picture, as honest as I can make it, with every source named.
  </p>

  <h2>What I am, and what I am not</h2>
  <p>
    I am not a therapist, a doctor, or an economist, and this site never
    pretends otherwise. I am someone who reads the underlying research
    carefully, checks figures against the original tables, hedges when the
    evidence is soft, and refuses to publish a number I cannot cite. Where a
    topic is genuinely clinical, the pages say so plainly and point to
    qualified professionals. Nothing here is medical, psychological,
    financial, or legal advice; the <a href="/disclaimer">disclaimer</a> spells
    that out.
  </p>

  <h2>How I make every page</h2>
  <p>
    Every page follows the same process, described in full on the
    <a href="/research-methodology">research methodology</a> page:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Start from a real, specific question people actually ask.</li>
    <li>Pull figures only from government statistics, large established surveys, and peer-reviewed studies. Never from anecdote, never invented.</li>
    <li>Hedge numbers honestly ("roughly," "about") and say plainly when research is mixed or weak.</li>
    <li>Separate what the data shows, why it feels different, what helps, and, just as important, what does not help.</li>
    <li>Name the sources on every page and keep a full <a href="/data-sources">source list</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The rules I will not break</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No invented numbers, ever.</strong> If I cannot cite it, it does not go on the page.</li>
    <li><strong>Context, not verdicts.</strong> I show the distribution. I do not grade your place in it.</li>
    <li><strong>Honest about uncertainty.</strong> Strong findings, weak findings, and open questions are labeled as what they are.</li>
    <li><strong>Humane on hard topics.</strong> Accurate and kind at the same time, and pointing to real help where someone may need it.</li>
    <li><strong>Privacy by design.</strong> The <a href="/assessment">assessment</a> runs entirely in your browser. Your answers are never stored or transmitted, and I could not read them if I wanted to.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Accuracy and corrections</h2>
  <p>
    I would genuinely rather hear about an error than not. Every page carries a
    "last reviewed" date, and figures get updated as new data is published. If
    something looks wrong or out of date, tell me on the
    <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. Include the page and, if you can, a
    source, and I will review and correct or annotate it.
  </p>

  <h2>How the site is funded</h2>
  <p>
    Keeping the library free costs money to research, write, and host. The site
    is supported by advertising and may display ads served through Google
    AdSense. Advertising never influences the content: which studies I cite and
    what I conclude are decided on the evidence alone. Ads are never placed
    inside the assessment or its results, and never next to a specific data
    finding. The <a href="/privacy-policy">privacy policy</a> explains how
    advertising cookies work and how to opt out.
  </p>

  <h2>Say hello</h2>
  <p>
    Questions, corrections, or a source you think I should use? Email
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> or use the
    <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. You can also read the
    <a href="/research-methodology">methodology</a>, browse
    <a href="/data-sources">every source</a>, or see the numbers themselves on
    <a href="/by-the-numbers">By the Numbers</a>.
  </p>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .author-card {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    background: var(--card-bg, #fff);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: var(--space-m) var(--space-l);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
  }
  .author-card img {
    width: 96px;
    height: 96px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }
  .author-card__name {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 0.2rem;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .author-card__role {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .author-card {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: var(--space-s);
    }
    .author-card img {
      width: 72px;
      height: 72px;
    }
  }
</style>
